# Optional HTTPS: built-in TLS, trusted proxy, secure cookie

## What to build

graph-manager (`apps/graph-manager`) today only speaks plain HTTP (`src/server/main.ts:49` `app.listen`, `src/server/app.ts:104` `Fastify({...})`), and its session cookie is never `Secure`. Phones need a secure origin (HTTPS) to install the app and run a service worker. This slice adds **optional** HTTPS, off by default:

1. **Built-in TLS.** New env vars `GM_TLS_CERT` and `GM_TLS_KEY` (and the CLI flags `--tls-cert <file>` / `--tls-key <file>` on `graph-execution manager`). When both are set, `main.ts` reads both files once at startup and passes `{ https: { cert, key } }` to `buildApp`, which hands it to `Fastify(...)`. Only one of the two set is an error: `set both GM_TLS_CERT and GM_TLS_KEY, or neither`.
2. **Trusted proxy.** New env var `GM_TRUST_PROXY` (`1`, `true`, `yes`, case-insensitive → on). It turns on Fastify's `trustProxy`, so `req.protocol` follows `X-Forwarded-Proto` and `req.ip` follows `X-Forwarded-For` (separate login rate-limit buckets per real client behind `tailscale serve` / Caddy).
3. **Secure cookie.** The login cookie gets `secure: req.protocol === "https"`, and `/api/auth/logout` clears it with the same `secure` value.
4. **Startup output.** The printed URL starts with `https://` when TLS is on; the line format is otherwise unchanged.
5. **Terminals keep working with TLS on.** The terminals' internal API URL (`GM_INTERNAL_URL`, used by the `graph-execution --sandbox` shim and the `graph-manager-mcp` tools) is plain `http://` today and would break on a TLS port. With TLS on, a second plain-HTTP listener on `127.0.0.1` (random port) serves only `/api/internal/*` and becomes `GM_INTERNAL_URL`.
6. **Docs.** README gets an **HTTPS setup (phones and installing the app)** section (Tailscale recommended, own certificate, Caddy), an **Install on a phone** paragraph, the new variables in the configuration tables, and the new flags in the flags tables.

Plain HTTP must stay exactly as it is when no TLS variable is set. Nothing changes in `originAllowed` (it already compares `Origin` with `Host` and `X-Forwarded-Host`), and the browser already picks `wss:` on `https:` (`src/web/components/TerminalView.tsx:27`). No Dockerfile change. Certificates are read once; a renewed one needs a restart (no hot reload, no ACME, no HTTP→HTTPS redirect).

